  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA lingering blanket of cirrus has helped cap temperatures in the \nlow/mid 30s early this afternoon. Though meteorological winter is \nstill a week away, the high and low temperatures expected through \nFriday morning are much more reminiscent of December than November. \nForecast highs for Wednesday and Thursday currently sit in the upper \n30s and lower 40s (around 5-10 degF cooler than normal).\n\nSnow is also in the forecast for the first time this season. A \nclosed upper-level low continues to work through the Northern Plains \ntoday with snowfall noted as far south as northeast Nebraska. As \nthis system surges southward, it is expected to interact with a \nsecondary upper-level low careening over the Southern Plains. In \nthis meeting, some level of phasing is expected to ensue. Guidance \nconcurs that the overall system will adopt an increasingly negative \ntilt over the course of the day on Wednesday. This orientation will \nhelp draw Gulf moisture poleward and into the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley. Precipitation is expected to begin across southeastern \nMissouri and southern Illinois in the early afternoon hours \ntomorrow. This will likely begin as rain or some type of wintry mix \nwith temperatures remaining above freezing, though wetbulbing could \nallow for a transition to snow well before dark in these areas. For \nour portions of Missouri, however, it is unlikely that we will see \nprecipitation of any kind until after dark when that plume of \nmoisture is first expected to wrap around the northern extent of the \nlow. Sub-freezing temperatures throughout the vertical profile \nleaves little question about the potential precipitation type \novernight Wednesday into early Thursday morning. This window of time \nis expected to be when most of the snowfall occurs; the dusk to dawn \nhours appear to be the only timeframe in which the temperature and \nmoisture profiles align for snow production. Although PoPs continue \nthroughout the day on Thursday, confidence in accumulating snowfall \nis much more limited as surface temperatures are expected to rise \nabove freezing once again. That said, if the above-surface layer \nremains above freezing, we could continue to see snowflakes/flurries \nThanksgiving Day...just probably not much in the way of \naccumulation. \n\nRecent guidance has increased snowfall chances back to the west, a \nreversal of overnight trends. As a result of this, the expected \nsnowfall totals have also increased further west. Using the US-65 \ncorridor as a reference point, areas to the east could see 1-2\" of \nsnowfall (25th-75th percentiles, respectively). Closer to US-65\nitself, slightly lesser (but still measurable) accumulations of\n0.5- 1\" seem more probable. Since moisture is slower to arrive \nfurther west, measurable snowfall remains unlikely for the \nKansas City metro area. Snowflakes and flurries on Thursday are \ncertainly not out of the question, but with warming \ntemperatures, it will be difficult to see much accumulation. \n\nA more zonal pattern settles in for the weekend with temperatures \nclimbing back into the 50s where they are expected to stay for at \nleast the early part of next week.",
